{% callout tag="TLDR" %}
The hype tells you the next model release, the next shiny tool, is what will make you productive. From my experience, it's not — **your codebase is**. Build solid foundations, iterate on your tools, do the boring work, and the investment pays off with any model and any AI tool you could pick. Best of all, **you stay in control**: the approach is agnostic, and it survives every release cycle. In my case, it accounts for 50%+ of my productivity gains — with better code and broader test coverage than I shipped before LLMs.
{% /callout %}

## It's not the model, it's the codebase

At the time I'm writing this, a consensus is forming: left alone, agents [aren't capable of writing maintainable code](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.03823), and the wrong technical decisions they make compound into a flaky codebase with [more bugs than a human would produce](https://www.coderabbit.ai/blog/state-of-ai-vs-human-code-generation-report). The result often just feels "off" — proof that subtle choices matter when you want a codebase that "feels good".

> The models definitely still make mistakes and if you have any code you actually care about I would watch them like a hawk, in a nice large IDE on the side.
> — Andrej Karpathy

So human reviews are still necessary. But reviews are expensive, tiring — especially on generated code — and human time is the scarce resource next to an agent's output. **Review is becoming the real bottleneck to the productivity boost LLMs promise.** The whole game is to speed it up without lowering the bar: spot the critical details fast, and trust that the mundane 95% was done exactly as you'd want, so you can skim it.

Yes, some of this will age poorly — agents will get better, multi-model review will get cheaper, discovery will improve. But **making all your agents code "the same way" will stay on topic.** Competent human teams have wrestled with that for decades; agents are no different. An LLM-made Frankenstein codebase is no better than a human-made one.

We crossed the tipping point months ago: "AI-assisted engineering" — as opposed to "vibe coding" — can produce well-crafted, documented code, **as long as you don't throw tasks beyond its current reach.** Once you treat LLMs like [polyglot junior devs who can't learn](/writing/developers-are-dead-long-live-lead-developers) — poor long-term memory, no real vision, but fast hands — and build a framework around those limits, you stop trading quality for velocity.

## Codebase context engineering

So how do you make every agent code the same way? Your first instinct is probably Claude Skills, Cursor rules, a multi-level prompt system, some elaborate knowledge base.

I tried that, and failed. I came to a simple conclusion: **nothing explains code better than code itself.** So I went the other way — bake as many rules as possible into the code and its structure.

That's **codebase context engineering** (I didn't coin the term). Instead of prose rules, it takes this shape:

- structure enforced by providing actual, well, code;
- tech choices enforced by setting things up yourself;
- detailed documentation that ships with the project;
- automation and process — hooks, CI, rules, skills — already in place;
- examples baked into the codebase.

Ultimately you want a repository you can clone, throw agents at, and trust they'll (almost) always produce the same quality.

**The *how* of all this is a series of its own** — [Codebase context engineering pt.1 - The silent foundations](/writing/codebase-context-engineering-pt-1-the-silent-foundations), Codebase context engineering pt.3 - Document the how and why, and enforce your process, and Codebase context engineering pt.4 - Show, don't tell — and what not to delegate. This piece is about the *why*, and about the one part the series can't give you: the judgment to know what to build, and what to never hand off.

## We built a boilerplate to find out

Being CEO at [Lonestone](https://lonestone.io) — a web development company — gave me the perfect testbed. We work for many clients; we automate more of the coding every month, but the complexity still demands real human involvement. We don't vibe-code. Our quality bar is high, and our projects can't become AI black boxes we no longer understand.

We'd wanted a shared codebase for years. A real, maintained boilerplate is expensive to build — but, like so much else, LLMs cut that cost enough that I finally started in 2025.

And I wasn't only chasing agent productivity. Our developers switch between projects constantly, and we kick off something new most months — a good boilerplate is a team multiplier first. That's the through-line: **what works well for humans works just as well for agents.**

After a few months solo, then a few more with the team, we [open-sourced it](https://lonestone.github.io/lonestone-boilerplate/). Our productivity skyrocketed, with no measurable hit to quality.

## What I won't delegate

We got that payoff for a reason: the decisions that mattered most, we made ourselves. Which brings me to the part I find most underrated.

Structuring the codebase, choosing the tech and frameworks, writing the documentation, writing the specs — **none of it should be delegated to an LLM.** These are the load-bearing decisions that let you automate everything else, and LLMs are bad at all of them.

Take picking and assembling technology. LLMs are weak at the choosing, and weaker still at the *plumbing* — wiring frameworks and tools together. Opus still misses trivial errors in a `tsconfig`; that's the moment you feel how little real intelligence is underneath.

It goes deeper:

- **Their taste is a popularity contest.** Choices reflect training volume, so you'll get an "OK" React stack and something much worse the moment you reach for a less mainstream library — or anything but the market leader.
- **Their knowledge is stale by construction.** Training lags reality, so they'll happily recommend an unmaintained library because there's a pile of old Stack Overflow about it. I stay picky about last-release dates and repo activity; **pinning the right version is manual work** too, especially across fast-moving, interdependent packages.
- **Trade-offs aren't legible to them.** The pros and cons of competing tech aren't obvious to an LLM — no more than to a junior.

And that's the point: **this has always been a core part of our job, the part you never outsourced to a junior. So don't outsource it to a machine** — one with none of your experience, context, or vision. Same goes for your structure, your lint rules, your commit hooks. Those strategic calls matter more as a project grows, and an LLM will happily over-engineer, or miss the abstraction that would've saved you months.

**Trust your experience. Use your judgment.**

## Go deeper, not broader

AI-assisted engineering is, in the end, about continuous improvement — like any semi-automated system, the more care you design into it, the more it returns.

We're in a period of constant churn: models and the tooling around them shift every week. I'm convinced the move is **not** to chase every trend, but to **build strong, agnostic foundations** that work with whatever Claude Code alternative shows up next. Instead of trashing everything each project, we improve on the same base — and every new project (at least one a week) is a chance to push its limits.

I genuinely believe AI should let us ship not just *faster*, but *better*. Keeping the same foundations lets us layer more on top — CI, scripts, deeper integrations like Sentry and Langfuse and how they feed back into the agents. **We go deeper, not broader — and I think that's the whole point.**

So go build your own toolbox. Iron it out, find the tools you actually want, and use them. Ship more complex, beautiful, performant apps — **not just more of them.**
